course: books
lesson: 46
title: The Wind in the Willows, chapter VI, part 2: Mr. Toad («I'm not sorry!» and the Toad locked in his bedroom)
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: «Talking to Toad'll never cure him,» says the Rat. «He'll say anything.» Through the door they hear the Badger's voice droning on and on, and then — long sobs from Toad. After three-quarters of an hour the Badger leads out a limp, tear-stained Toad: he is sorry, the Badger announces, and has promised to give up motor-cars for ever.
explain: But the Rat thinks he sees a twinkle in Toad's sorrowful eye. The Badger asks Toad to repeat his promise in front of his friends. A long, long pause… then: «No! I'm not sorry. And it wasn't folly at all! It was simply glorious!» In there, Toad explains, the Badger was so moving that he'd have said anything. And the first car he sees — poop-poop! — off he goes in it.
explain: «Told you so,» says the Rat to the Mole. So the Badger decides on force: the three of them will move into Toad Hall and stay until Toad is cured. They haul him upstairs, kicking, and lock him in his bedroom, promising it's all for his own good, while Toad shouts rude names through the keyhole.
explain: The friends take turns guarding him day and night. At first Toad has wild fits: he lines up bedroom chairs like a motor-car, crouches on the front one making engine noises, and ends by somersaulting into the wreck of chairs, quite satisfied. Slowly the fits come less often — but Toad grows dull, droopy and sad.
fact: The Rat: «That's no good! Talking to Toad'll never cure him. He'll say anything.»
fact: They hear «the long continuous drone of the Badger's voice, rising and falling in waves of oratory» (oratory = speech-making); then «long-drawn sobs» from Toad, «a soft-hearted and affectionate fellow, very easily converted—for the time being—to any point of view».
fact: «After some three-quarters of an hour» the Badger leads out «a very limp and dejected Toad» (dejected = low, miserable): «his legs wobbled, and his cheeks were furrowed by the tears».
fact: The Badger: «Toad has at last seen the error of his ways … he has undertaken (promised) to give up motor-cars entirely and for ever.»
fact: The Rat, «dubiously» (doubtfully): «Very good news indeed, if only—if only——». He sees «something vaguely resembling a twinkle in that animal's still sorrowful eye».
fact: After «a long, long pause» Toad says «a little sullenly, but stoutly» (sullenly = sulkily; stoutly = firmly): «No! I'm not sorry. And it wasn't folly at all! It was simply glorious!»
fact: «You backsliding animal» cries the Badger (backsliding = slipping back into bad ways).
fact: Toad: «I'd have said anything in there. You're so eloquent, dear Badger … you can do what you like with me in there … But I've been searching my mind since … I find that I'm not a bit sorry or repentant really».
fact: «On the contrary, I faithfully promise that the very first motor-car I see, poop-poop! off I go in it!» The Rat to the Mole: «Told you so, didn't I?»
fact: The Badger: «Since you won't yield to persuasion, we'll try what force can do … You've often asked us three to come and stay with you, Toad … well, now we're going to.»
fact: The Rat: «It's for your own good, Toady.» The Mole: «we'll see your money isn't wasted». The Rat: «No more of those regrettable incidents with the police».
fact: The Mole: «And no more weeks in hospital, being ordered about by female nurses». That is an old idea — that it is shameful for a man to be told what to do by women. Today nurses can be women or men, and listening to them is just sensible.
fact: The Badger: «He must never be left an instant unguarded … till the poison has worked itself out of his system». They «arranged watches» — taking turns, sleeping in Toad's room at night.
fact: In his «violent paroxysms» (fits) Toad would «arrange bedroom chairs in rude resemblance of a motor-car», crouch on the front one «making uncouth and ghastly noises», then turn «a complete somersault» and lie «amidst the ruins of the chairs, apparently completely satisfied».
fact: Later «these painful seizures grew gradually less frequent», but Toad «grew apparently languid and depressed» (languid = droopy, without energy).
fact: Talk in this lesson: «bruh» = mate, come on; «bummer» (older US) = what a pity; «don't have a cow» (older US) = don't get so upset; «howay» (North East England) = come on; «tamping» (Wales) = furious.
exercise: What does the Rat think of the Badger's plan to talk to Toad? | It's no good — Toad will say anything | It's the best plan, and Toad will be cured in an hour | He doesn't care | He wants to join in
exercise: What do Rat and Mole hear through the door? | The Badger's voice droning, then Toad sobbing | Toad shouting and throwing furniture about the room | Silence | Laughter
exercise: Why does Toad sob, according to the book? | He is soft-hearted and easily won over — for the time being | The Badger is hitting him with a big stick behind the closed door | He has hurt his foot | He is laughing so hard
exercise: How long is Toad in the smoking-room with the Badger? | About three-quarters of an hour | Five minutes, no more than that | All day and all night | Two hours
exercise: How does Toad look when he comes out? | Limp, wobbly and tear-stained | Proud and grinning, with his goggles on again | Angry, stamping and red in the face | Fast asleep, snoring on the leather sofa
exercise: What does the Badger announce? | Toad has promised to give up motor-cars for ever | Toad is going away on a long holiday across the sea | Toad will sell Toad Hall | Toad needs a doctor
exercise: What does the Rat notice in Toad's eye? | Something like a twinkle | A real tear of true sorrow | A speck of dust from the road | Nothing at all — his eyes are shut
exercise: What does the Badger ask Toad to do in front of his friends? | Repeat that he's sorry and sees his folly | Shake hands with each of them and say thank you | Sing a song | Sign a paper
exercise: How long is the pause before Toad answers? | Long, long | Not a second | Exactly a minute | He never answers
exercise: What is Toad's answer? | «No! I'm not sorry … It was simply glorious!» | «Yes, I am truly sorry, and I promise again» | «I don't remember» | «Ask the Rat»
exercise: «You backsliding animal!» What does «backsliding» mean? | Slipping back into bad ways | Falling down the stairs on your back | Driving a car in reverse gear | Telling lies about other people
exercise: Why did Toad say sorry «in there»? | The Badger was so moving he'd have said anything | He really meant it at the time, and he still does now | The Badger paid him | He was tired
exercise: What does Toad «faithfully promise» instead? | To jump in the very first motor-car he sees | To give up motor-cars, but only for one year and a day | To be good | To sell his cars
exercise: What does the Rat say to the Mole? | «Told you so, didn't I?» | «Poor old Toad, it's not his fault at all» | «Let's all go home for tea» | «Well I never, how lovely!»
exercise: What does the Badger decide, since talking has failed? | To try force: they'll stay at Toad Hall till he's cured | To give up on Toad and go back to the wood for good and never return | To call the police | To buy the car
exercise: Where do they lock Toad up? | In his bedroom | In the cellar under the banqueting-hall | In the boat-house | In the coach-house
exercise: What does the Rat say as they haul Toad upstairs? | «It's for your own good, Toady» | «You deserve this, you silly ass» | «Goodbye for ever» | Nothing at all
exercise: What does the Mole promise to look after? | Toad's money, so it isn't wasted | Toad's motor-cars, keeping them polished | Toad's boats down in the boat-house | Toad's big garden and its green lawns
exercise: What does Toad do once he's locked in? | Shouts abuse through the keyhole | Sings happily to himself and goes to sleep | Cries quietly into his pillow alone | Reads a big book about motor-cars in bed
exercise: What are «watches» here? | Turns at guarding Toad | Clocks you wear on your wrist | Things you look at through a window | Night-time walks round the garden
exercise: Why must Toad never be left alone, says the Badger? | Till the «poison» has worked out of his system | Because he is afraid of the dark at night and cries | Because he is ill | Because he is lonely
exercise: What does Toad build in his bedroom during his fits? | A pretend motor-car out of chairs | A boat out of his bed and sheets and pillows | A wall | A ladder
exercise: How does each fit end? | A somersault into the wreck of the chairs | Toad crying and saying sorry to all his friends | A nap | A song
exercise: How does Toad change as time passes? | Fewer fits — but droopy and sad | More fits, wilder every single day | Happy and busy | Exactly the same
test: Ben: «Toad said sorry to everyone and meant it, bruh.» (bruh = come on, mate) Put it right. | He said it in the smoking-room, then took it back in front of his friends | Right: he meant every single word and he never touched a motor-car again | He never said sorry at all, even to the Badger | He said sorry only to the Rat
test: Is this TRUE or FALSE? «The Rat expected Toad to keep his promise.» | False: he doubted it from the start and saw a twinkle | True: the Rat trusted Toad's promise completely and shook his paw | True, and he cheered | False: the Rat wasn't there
test: Toad says: «I find that I'm not a bit sorry … so it's no earthly good saying I am.» Is there anything good in this? | He is honest about not being sorry — though that's not good news | No, he is lying again, as he always does to everyone | Yes, he is sorry after all | He means he is sorry
test: Chloe: «locking Toad up is a bummer, but it's to keep him safe, right?» (bummer = a pity) Why do the friends say they're doing it? | For his own good — to stop crashes, police and wasted money | To take Toad Hall and all its money for themselves | Because they're cross | As a game
test: «Being ordered about by female nurses.» What do we say about this old idea today? | It's wrong to think being helped by women is shameful; nurses are men or women | It's true that only men should ever be allowed to give orders in a hospital | Nurses shouldn't give orders | It's about the hospital food
test: Looking back: in chapter IV, which line about Toad did the Badger say in winter? | «We'll make him be a sensible Toad» | «We'll leave him alone, he'll learn by himself in the end, you'll see» | «Toad is perfect» | «I'll buy him a horse»
test: Looking back: in chapter IV, the Badger «never said, “I told you so”». Who says «Told you so» now? | The Rat | The Badger | The Mole | Toad
test: Looking back: in chapter II, after the first car, how did the Rat explain Toad's state? | He is possessed; a new craze takes him like that at first | He is ill and needs a doctor and a nurse at once | He is pretending | He is asleep
test: Looking back: in chapter II, what did Toad call boating, once he had his caravan? | «Silly boyish amusement» | «The only thing worth doing in the whole wide world» | «The poetry of motion» | «Good fun»
test: Looking back: in chapter I, the Rat said everyone had to stay with Toad on what last year? | His house-boat | His caravan | His motor-car | His island
test: «You've often asked us three to come and stay with you, Toad … well, now we're going to.» Why is this funny? | Toad asked them as guests; now they come as guards | The friends never visit anyone at all, not even each other | Toad has no spare beds | The Badger hates visiting
test: Looking back: in chapter II, what did Toad say about the cart right after the crash? | «I've done with carts for ever» | «I'll mend it tomorrow and paint it again» | «Poor cart!» | «Let's buy two more»
test: What would happen if they left Toad unguarded for an afternoon? | He might escape and find a car | He would sit quietly in his room and read a book | He would visit the Badger | Nothing
test: Darren from Newcastle: «howay, he's just playing cars with chairs, it's dead harmless.» (howay = come on) What do the chair-cars show about Toad? | The craze still has hold of him, like an illness | He has a lot of fun with the chairs and is cured by it | He wants to be a carpenter | He is bored of cars
test: ★★★ The Rat says «He'll say anything». Toad says «I'd have said anything in there». What does that tell us about Toad's promises? | They depend on who is talking to him, so they can't be trusted | They are always true, every single one | He never makes promises | He only lies to the Rat
test: ★★★ «Converted—for the time being—to any point of view.» Which phrase warns us the change won't last? | «for the time being» | «any point of view» | «converted» | «soft-hearted»
test: ★★★ «A long, long pause. Toad looked desperately this way and that.» Why might he be desperate before saying «No»? | He's deciding whether to lie again or tell the truth | He has forgotten what the question was and wants it asked again | He is looking for the door | He is looking for his goggles
exam: Identify the duration of the interview between the Badger and Toad in the smoking-room. | Some three-quarters of an hour | Upwards of three hours, with intervals | Some five minutes | A full day
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises Toad's declaration before his friends? | A retraction of his professed repentance | A solemn repetition of the undertaking given in private to the Badger | A refusal to speak | An apology to the Rat
exam: Ascertain the measure adopted by the Badger upon the failure of persuasion. | The confinement and guarding of Toad within Toad Hall | The summoning of the police, and the prosecution of Toad for his offences | The sale of the motor-car | Departure from Toad Hall
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the course of Toad's «paroxysms». | They grew less frequent, while he grew languid and depressed | They increased in frequency and in violence day by day, without any remission | They ceased at once | They never occurred
